1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,120
A gardener takes cuttings from one hydrangea, so

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:05,840
every new plant is a clone, with exactly

3
00:00:05,920 --> 00:00:07,080
the same genes.

4
00:00:07,360 --> 00:00:10,560
Planted in two different gardens, one flowers blue,

5
00:00:10,640 --> 00:00:12,200
and one flowers pink.

6
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:15,000
Last time, a ribosome built a chain of

7
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:16,320
amino acids.

8
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:18,480
So how does a chain like that become

9
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:20,560
a trait you can see, and how can

10
00:00:20,640 --> 00:00:22,880
the same genes give different results?

11
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:26,100
Most of a cell's jobs are done by

12
00:00:26,260 --> 00:00:27,260
proteins.

13
00:00:27,540 --> 00:00:29,780
Enzymes speed up reactions.

14
00:00:30,020 --> 00:00:34,180
Transport proteins carry things, like haemoglobin carrying oxygen

15
00:00:34,220 --> 00:00:35,460
in red blood cells.

16
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:38,740
Structural proteins give strength, like the keratin in

17
00:00:38,820 --> 00:00:39,540
your hair.

18
00:00:39,780 --> 00:00:42,740
Receptors on a cell's surface pick up signals,

19
00:00:42,820 --> 00:00:45,180
and antibodies grab onto germs.

20
00:00:45,460 --> 00:00:48,420
Every one of these is a polypeptide chain,

21
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:50,100
folded into its own shape.

22
00:00:51,300 --> 00:00:53,540
So why does a chain fold into one

23
00:00:53,620 --> 00:00:54,820
particular shape?

24
00:00:55,060 --> 00:00:57,860
Because the twenty amino acids are different.

25
00:00:58,060 --> 00:01:00,420
Some avoid water, so they tuck into the

26
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:02,580
middle of the chain, away from the watery

27
00:01:02,660 --> 00:01:03,460
cell.

28
00:01:03,620 --> 00:01:07,060
Some carry a charge, and opposite charges attract.

29
00:01:07,180 --> 00:01:09,780
And two amino acids called cysteine can lock

30
00:01:09,860 --> 00:01:12,580
together with a strong link called a disulfide

31
00:01:12,660 --> 00:01:13,460
bond.

32
00:01:13,540 --> 00:01:16,100
The order of amino acids decides which parts

33
00:01:16,180 --> 00:01:18,180
end up next to each other, so it

34
00:01:18,260 --> 00:01:19,380
decides the shape.

35
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:24,160
Shape matters because proteins work by fitting.

36
00:01:24,440 --> 00:01:27,400
An enzyme's substrate fits into a pocket, its

37
00:01:27,560 --> 00:01:29,960
active site, like a key in a lock,

38
00:01:30,040 --> 00:01:31,720
though the protein can flex a little as

39
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:32,520
it binds.

40
00:01:33,160 --> 00:01:35,640
Change one amino acid in that pocket, and

41
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:38,040
the shape can change so the substrate no

42
00:01:38,120 --> 00:01:39,160
longer fits.

43
00:01:39,480 --> 00:01:41,320
But not every change matters.

44
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:43,800
A swap far from the pocket, or to

45
00:01:43,880 --> 00:01:46,200
a similar amino acid, may leave the shape

46
00:01:46,280 --> 00:01:46,840
working.

47
00:01:47,240 --> 00:01:49,720
And many base changes don't change the amino

48
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:50,680
acid at all.

49
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:54,460
Let's follow one real case all the way.

50
00:01:55,020 --> 00:01:57,740
Haemoglobin is built from four chains.

51
00:01:57,980 --> 00:01:59,500
In the gene for one of them, the

52
00:01:59,580 --> 00:02:02,380
beta chain, a single base change turns the

53
00:02:02,460 --> 00:02:05,660
codon G A G into G U G

54
00:02:05,820 --> 00:02:07,180
in the mRNA.

55
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:10,220
The chart gives valine instead of glutamate, at

56
00:02:10,260 --> 00:02:11,500
the sixth amino acid.

57
00:02:12,140 --> 00:02:15,180
So one base changes one amino acid, out

58
00:02:15,260 --> 00:02:17,660
of a chain of a hundred and forty-six.

59
00:02:18,867 --> 00:02:22,906
Glutamate carries a charge, but valine avoids water.

60
00:02:23,187 --> 00:02:25,026
So in a person with two copies of

61
00:02:25,107 --> 00:02:28,146
this allele, when oxygen is low, the changed

62
00:02:28,227 --> 00:02:31,106
haemoglobin molecules stick to each other and stack

63
00:02:31,267 --> 00:02:33,426
into long stiff fibres.

64
00:02:33,507 --> 00:02:35,506
The fibres bend the red blood cells into

65
00:02:35,587 --> 00:02:37,026
a sickle shape.

66
00:02:37,067 --> 00:02:39,586
Sickle cells are stiff, so they block small

67
00:02:39,707 --> 00:02:42,546
blood vessels and cause pain, and they break

68
00:02:42,707 --> 00:02:44,786
down quickly, so there are too few red

69
00:02:45,227 --> 00:02:45,746
cells.

70
00:02:45,827 --> 00:02:48,546
That whole pattern is the trait, sickle cell

71
00:02:48,587 --> 00:02:49,266
disease.

72
00:02:50,467 --> 00:02:52,386
Notice how many steps that took.

73
00:02:52,947 --> 00:02:55,026
Genes don't become traits directly.

74
00:02:55,507 --> 00:02:58,226
A gene codes for a polypeptide, which folds

75
00:02:58,307 --> 00:03:00,786
into a protein, which changes what a cell

76
00:03:00,867 --> 00:03:03,906
does, and that shows up as a trait.

77
00:03:04,027 --> 00:03:06,626
Your set of alleles is your genotype.

78
00:03:06,707 --> 00:03:08,786
The traits you can observe, from a cell's

79
00:03:08,987 --> 00:03:10,866
shape to the colour of a flower, are

80
00:03:10,947 --> 00:03:12,306
your phenotype.

81
00:03:12,427 --> 00:03:15,426
And most traits involve many proteins, so many

82
00:03:15,587 --> 00:03:16,946
genes, at once.

83
00:03:18,167 --> 00:03:20,046
Now back to the hydrangeas.

84
00:03:20,327 --> 00:03:22,566
Their genes code for the enzymes that make

85
00:03:22,647 --> 00:03:24,406
a red pigment in the flowers.

86
00:03:24,967 --> 00:03:28,166
In acidic soil, aluminium dissolves, and the roots

87
00:03:28,287 --> 00:03:28,806
take it up.

88
00:03:29,367 --> 00:03:32,166
In the flowers, aluminium binds to the pigment

89
00:03:32,327 --> 00:03:33,366
and turns it blue.

90
00:03:34,087 --> 00:03:37,526
In less acidic soil, little aluminium dissolves, so

91
00:03:37,607 --> 00:03:39,286
the same pigment stays pink.

92
00:03:39,927 --> 00:03:41,606
The genotype is the same.

93
00:03:41,927 --> 00:03:44,326
The environment changes what it produces.

94
00:03:45,047 --> 00:03:46,566
Height works the same way.

95
00:03:46,967 --> 00:03:49,606
It depends on many genes, but children who

96
00:03:49,687 --> 00:03:51,846
don't get enough food don't reach the height

97
00:03:51,927 --> 00:03:53,126
their genes allow.

98
00:03:54,334 --> 00:03:56,733
So how much is genes, and how much

99
00:03:56,774 --> 00:03:57,613
is environment?

100
00:03:58,254 --> 00:04:01,373
Genes set what's possible, and the environment affects

101
00:04:01,494 --> 00:04:04,013
what actually develops within that range.

102
00:04:04,254 --> 00:04:06,493
The balance differs between traits.

103
00:04:06,734 --> 00:04:08,813
Your ABO blood group is set by your

104
00:04:08,894 --> 00:04:11,773
alleles, and food or climate won't change it.

105
00:04:12,254 --> 00:04:14,013
Height depends on both.

106
00:04:14,254 --> 00:04:16,813
And the environment changes the trait, not the

107
00:04:16,974 --> 00:04:18,093
genes themselves.

108
00:04:18,334 --> 00:04:20,173
Take a cutting from the blue plant and

109
00:04:20,254 --> 00:04:22,413
grow it in less acidic soil, and its

110
00:04:22,494 --> 00:04:24,013
new flowers come out pink.

111
00:04:25,234 --> 00:04:28,273
That's why making polypeptides matters so much.

112
00:04:28,754 --> 00:04:31,393
Almost everything a cell does depends on its

113
00:04:31,474 --> 00:04:35,153
proteins, and every protein is made by transcription

114
00:04:35,234 --> 00:04:36,473
and translation.

115
00:04:36,834 --> 00:04:39,633
Your cells all carry the same genes, but

116
00:04:39,714 --> 00:04:41,793
each kind of cell makes its own set

117
00:04:41,834 --> 00:04:43,953
of proteins, which is why a red blood

118
00:04:44,034 --> 00:04:46,433
cell and a muscle cell look and work

119
00:04:46,594 --> 00:04:47,393
so differently.

120
00:04:48,034 --> 00:04:49,953
If the sequence is wrong, or the protein

121
00:04:50,034 --> 00:04:52,673
isn't made, the job doesn't get done.

122
00:04:53,900 --> 00:04:54,780
Two aids.

123
00:04:55,180 --> 00:04:58,780
For protein structure: one, the order of amino

124
00:04:58,860 --> 00:05:03,900
acids; two, local coils and folds; three,

125
00:05:03,940 --> 00:05:07,500
the whole folded shape of one chain; four,

126
00:05:07,740 --> 00:05:10,460
more than one chain together, like haemoglobin.

127
00:05:10,940 --> 00:05:14,220
And for traits: genes set what's possible, and

128
00:05:14,300 --> 00:05:17,100
the environment affects what actually develops.

129
00:05:18,300 --> 00:05:19,900
Two harder questions now.

130
00:05:20,380 --> 00:05:23,420
First, Himalayan rabbits have white fur on the

131
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:26,780
body, but dark fur on the ears, nose,

132
00:05:26,820 --> 00:05:27,820
feet and tail.

133
00:05:28,460 --> 00:05:30,860
The dark pigment is made by an enzyme.

134
00:05:31,740 --> 00:05:34,300
Scientists shaved a patch on one rabbit's back

135
00:05:34,540 --> 00:05:36,060
and kept an ice pack on it while

136
00:05:36,100 --> 00:05:37,100
the fur grew back.

137
00:05:37,660 --> 00:05:39,340
The new fur grew dark.

138
00:05:39,900 --> 00:05:42,060
Explain why the new fur is dark.

139
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:45,260
Then predict the back fur of this rabbit's

140
00:05:45,420 --> 00:05:48,700
young, raised normally, and justify your prediction.

141
00:05:49,020 --> 00:05:50,380
Pause here to answer.

142
00:06:00,800 --> 00:06:02,880
The dark parts are the coolest parts of

143
00:06:02,920 --> 00:06:05,360
the body, so the enzyme must work only

144
00:06:05,440 --> 00:06:07,120
where the skin is cool.

145
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:09,360
On the warm back it doesn't work, so

146
00:06:09,440 --> 00:06:11,040
no pigment forms.

147
00:06:11,080 --> 00:06:13,360
The ice pack cooled the skin, the enzyme

148
00:06:13,440 --> 00:06:15,720
worked, and the fur grew dark.

149
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,080
The young will have white backs, because the

150
00:06:18,160 --> 00:06:20,920
cold changed where the enzyme worked, not the

151
00:06:20,960 --> 00:06:22,200
gene they inherit.

152
00:06:22,480 --> 00:06:24,480
If you said the ice changed the rabbit's

153
00:06:24,720 --> 00:06:26,160
genes, look again.

154
00:06:27,367 --> 00:06:27,926
Second.

155
00:06:28,407 --> 00:06:31,686
In snapdragons, red petals need an enzyme that

156
00:06:31,767 --> 00:06:33,126
helps make red pigment.

157
00:06:33,687 --> 00:06:36,086
Two plants each carry two copies of a

158
00:06:36,247 --> 00:06:38,406
different change in this enzyme's gene.

159
00:06:38,887 --> 00:06:42,246
In plant P, the mRNA codon for an

160
00:06:42,327 --> 00:06:44,966
amino acid in the active site changes from

161
00:06:45,127 --> 00:06:47,806
C A U to C C U.

162
00:06:48,087 --> 00:06:50,566
In plant Q, a codon far from the

163
00:06:50,647 --> 00:06:53,606
active site changes from G A A to

164
00:06:53,767 --> 00:06:55,126
G A G.

165
00:06:55,447 --> 00:06:58,406
Plant P has white flowers, and plant Q

166
00:06:58,487 --> 00:06:59,606
has red flowers.

167
00:07:00,167 --> 00:07:02,006
Use the chart to explain both.

168
00:07:02,487 --> 00:07:03,766
Pause here to answer.

169
00:07:14,167 --> 00:07:17,286
In plant P, C A U is histidine

170
00:07:17,527 --> 00:07:20,086
and C C U is proline, so a

171
00:07:20,247 --> 00:07:22,806
different amino acid sits in the active site.

172
00:07:23,047 --> 00:07:25,846
The pocket's shape changes, the enzyme can't hold

173
00:07:25,927 --> 00:07:28,886
its substrate, and no red pigment forms, so

174
00:07:28,927 --> 00:07:30,126
the flowers are white.

175
00:07:30,407 --> 00:07:33,206
In plant Q, G A A and G

176
00:07:33,287 --> 00:07:36,086
A G both code for glutamate, so the

177
00:07:36,167 --> 00:07:39,046
enzyme is unchanged and the flowers stay red.

178
00:07:39,367 --> 00:07:41,446
If you said every change to a gene

179
00:07:41,527 --> 00:07:44,406
changes the protein, check the chart first.

180
00:07:45,634 --> 00:07:47,313
So when a question links a gene to

181
00:07:47,394 --> 00:07:50,673
a trait, walk the chain: the base, the

182
00:07:50,754 --> 00:07:53,993
codon, the amino acid, the shape, what the

183
00:07:54,034 --> 00:07:56,233
cell does, then the trait.

184
00:07:56,514 --> 00:07:59,153
And when the same genes give different results,

185
00:07:59,234 --> 00:08:01,553
look for what the environment is changing.

186
00:08:01,874 --> 00:08:04,753
Next, where the different genotypes in one family

187
00:08:04,834 --> 00:08:06,353
come from in the first place.

188
00:08:07,567 --> 00:08:09,006
Here are the key notes.

189
00:08:09,247 --> 00:08:11,166
Pause the video if you need more time

190
00:08:11,247 --> 00:08:12,046
to copy them.
